AdditionalAttributes[42]{Name,Description,DataType}:
  FLIGHT_LINE,Flight line identifies where the aircraft/instrument has flown to acquire data.,STRING
  GROUP_ID,ID that helps ASF group products,STRING
  OFF_NADIR_ANGLE,The angle the sensor points away from the satellite's nadir (off to the side). Larger angles correspond to imaging farther away from the satellite.,FLOAT
  MD5SUM,Checksum,STRING
  GRANULE_TYPE,"Identifies the type of data by combining platform, beam mode type, and coverage (frame/swath)",STRING
  ASCENDING_DESCENDING,"Describes whether the satellite travel direction was ascending towards the north pole, or descending towards the south pole.",STRING
  FAR_END_LAT,"Latitude of the granule where imaging ended farthest from the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the upper right corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  INSAR_STACK_SIZE,The number of SAR images over the same location in the entire ASF archive. Used for data that may be used for interferometry.,INT
  BEAM_MODE_TYPE,"In most cases, the BEAM_MODE_TYPE value is the same as the BEAM_MODE attribute. Each platform has its own scheme for defining acquisition sensor beam modes. Beam modes often reflect a set configuration of polarization, resolution, and off-nadir pointing angle.",STRING
  INSAR_BASELINE,The perpendicular distance between two satellites when they took the related two images. Useful for identifying image pairs to use for interferometry.,FLOAT
  CENTER_FRAME_ID,ID of the center frame,STRING
  CENTER_ESA_FRAME,The European Space Agency equivalent to the value in the FRAME_NUMBER field,INT
  ACQUISITION_DATE,Date the data was acquired by the satellite or aircraft and instrument.,STRING
  MISSION_NAME,"The sitenames where AIRSAR, AIRMOSS, and UAVSAR acquisitions were taken.",STRING
  CENTER_LON,Longitude of the center of the scene or frame.,FLOAT
  NEAR_START_LAT,"Latitude of the granule where imaging began nearest to the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the lower left corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  BEAM_MODE,"The general type of active sensor used. Values indicate image resolution: such as standard, fine, wide, scansar wide, SM (strip mode), EW (extra wide). Or indicate resolution + polarization: FBS (fine beam single polarization), FBD (fine beam dual pol). Or atypically the type of product: TOPSAR (high resolution DEM), RPI (repeat pass interferometry), XTI (across track interferometry). Or resolution and product type IW (interferometric wide).",STRING
  BEAM_MODE_DESC,Text that describes the beam mode,STRING
  PROCESSING_TYPE,"Indicates the type of product, such as browse, metadata, L1, complex, amplitude, projected, terrain corrected.",STRING
  PROCESSING_DESCRIPTION,"Describes what the data was processed to. For example: low resolution terrain corrected ALOS data.",STRING
  FRAME_NUMBER,"The designated frame or scene as defined by various space agencies (ESA, JAXA, CSA) each of which have their own scheme. The frame describes a box over the Earth at a fixed latitude. Combined with a path number (for longitude it identifies a specific piece of ground).",INT
  PROCESSING_LEVEL,"Indicates how much processing has been done to the data: L1, L1.0 unprocessed; L1.1 single look complex; L1, L1.5 amplitude image; L2, L2.2 terrain corrected image.",STRING
  PROCESSING_DATE,The date the data was processed,STRING
  NEAR_START_LON,"Longitude of the granule where imaging began nearest the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the lower left corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  DOPPLER,The doppler centroid frequency - Useful for SAR processing and interferometry. Related to the squint of the satellite.,FLOAT
  FAR_START_LAT,"Latitude of the granule where imaging began farthest from the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the lower right corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  NEAR_END_LON,"Longitude of the granule where imaging ended nearest to the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the upper left corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  PROCESSING_TYPE_DISPLAY,Label that will be displayed in ASF's Vertex interface,STRING
  POLARIZATION,"Radar transmissions are polarized, with components normally termed vertical (V) and horizontal (H). Vertical means that the electric vector is in the plane of incidence; horizontal means the electric vector is perpendicular to the plane of incidence.",STRING
  FAR_START_LON,"Latitude of the granule where imaging began farthest from the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the lower right corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  THUMBNAIL_URL,URL that points to this granule's thumbnail image,STRING
  ASF_PLATFORM,Internal ASF platform name,STRING
  INSAR_STACK_ID,An ASF-assigned unique number used internally to unify SAR images over the same location. Used for data that might be used for interferometry.,INT
  LOOK_DIRECTION,SAR imagery is taken with the sensor pointing either left or right of the satellite. Most data is right-looking.,STRING
  PATH_NUMBER,The path describes a path the satellite will follow over the earth similar to longitude; each path has an assigned number. Combined with a frame number it identifies a specific piece of ground.,INT
  NEAR_END_LAT,"Latitude of the granule where imaging ended nearest to the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the upper left corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  FARADAY_ROTATION,An effect of the ionosphere that rotates polarizations from HH or VV or HV or VH. Significant for L or P band. Rotation over 5 degrees reduces usefulness for applications such as forest biomass. Effect is reduced at solar minimum.,STRING
  FAR_END_LON,"Longitude of the granule where imaging ended farthest from the satellite. For an ascending satellite, it locates the upper right corner of a north-oriented image.",FLOAT
  BYTES,Product size in bytes.,FLOAT
  CENTER_LAT,Latitude of the center of the scene or frame.,FLOAT
  ARCHIVE_DATATAKE_ID,The ARCHIVE_DATATAKE_ID for this collection,STRING
  DEM_REGION,The DEM_REGION for this collection,STRING
